      "markdown": "which holds for  $ 0 \\leq x < 1 $, we find that\n\n $$ \\log\\zeta(s)=\\log\\prod_{p}\\frac{1}{1-p^{-s}}=\\sum_{p}\\log\\left(\\frac{1}{1-p^{-s}}\\right)=\\sum_{p,m}\\frac{p^{-m s}}{m}. $$ \n\nSince the double sum converges absolutely, we need not specify the order of summation. See the Note at the end of this chapter. The formula then holds for all  $ \\mathrm{Re}(s) > 1 $ by analytic continuation. Note that, by Theorem 6.2 in Chapter 3,  $ \\log \\zeta(s) $ is well defined in the simply connected half-plane  $ \\mathrm{Re}(s) > 1 $, since  $ \\zeta $ has no zeros there. Finally, it is clear that we have\n\n $$ \\sum_{p,m}\\frac{p^{-m s}}{m}=\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}c_{n}n^{-s}, $$ \n\nwhere  $ c_{n}=1/m $ if  $ n=p^{m} $ and  $ c_{n}=0 $ otherwise.\n\nThe proof of the theorem we shall give depends on a simple trick that is based on the following inequality.\n\nLemma 1.4 If  $ \\theta \\in \\mathbb{R} $, then  $ 3 + 4 \\cos \\theta + \\cos 2\\theta \\geq 0 $.\n\nThis follows at once from the simple observation\n\n $$ 3+4\\cos\\theta+\\cos2\\theta=2(1+\\cos\\theta)^{2}. $$ \n\nCorollary 1.5 If  $ \\sigma > 1 $ and t is real, then\n\n $$ \\log|\\zeta^{3}(\\sigma)\\zeta^{4}(\\sigma+i t)\\zeta(\\sigma+2i t)|\\geq0. $$ \n\nProof. Let  $ s = \\sigma + it $ and note that\n\n $$ \\mathrm{R e}(n^{-s})=\\mathrm{R e}(e^{-(\\sigma+i t)\\log n})=e^{-\\sigma\\log n}\\cos(t\\log n)=n^{-\\sigma}\\cos(t\\log n). $$ \n\nTherefore,\n\n $$ \\begin{aligned}&\\log\\left|\\zeta^{3}(\\sigma)\\zeta^{4}(\\sigma+it)\\zeta(\\sigma+2it)\\right|\\\\=&3\\log\\left|\\zeta(\\sigma)\\right|+4\\log\\left|\\zeta(\\sigma+it)\\right|+\\log\\left|\\zeta(\\sigma+2it)\\right|\\\\=&3Re[\\log\\zeta(\\sigma)]+4Re[\\log\\zeta(\\sigma+it)]+Re[\\log\\zeta(\\sigma+2it)]\\\\=&\\sum c_{n}n^{-\\sigma}(3+4\\cos\\theta_{n}+\\cos2\\theta_{n}),\\end{aligned} $$ \n\nwhere  $ \\theta_n = t \\log n $. The positivity now follows from Lemma 1.4, and the fact that  $ c_n \\geq 0 $.",
